\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Compact scalar frames preserve product probability}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Use the circle orbit of #{U(1)} or the unit sphere orbit of
#{SU(2)} with the normalized group and orbit probabilities of
\ref{fdip-010I}. The free orbit map #{g\mapsto g\phi^\circ} is
an equivariant homeomorphism. In the coordinates consisting of
all site frames and reduced links, the product reference
probability is product Haar on #{G^X\times G^E}. For a
continuous real joint weight of the stated invariant form and
a continuous gauge-invariant real observable #{f},
##{\begin{aligned}
 Z &= \int_{G^E}\check\Xi(V)\,\extd\mu_E(V),\\
 \int \Xi f\,\extd(\mu_E\otimes\mu_X)
   &= \int_{G^E}\check\Xi(V)\check f(V)\,\extd\mu_E(V).
\end{aligned}}
Here #{\check f} is the observable at the constant scalar.
The normalized integrals agree when #{Z\ne0}. A nonnegative
weight and #{Z>0} give the corresponding probability averages.}

\p{The explicit continuous inverse of the orbit map for each
group is given in \ref{fdip-010I}; equivariance is associativity
of the action. Thus changing the site field to its frames pulls
the orbit probability back to normalized Haar. The joint
coordinate map and its inverse, with
#{U_e=g_{h(e)}V_e g_{s(e)}^{-1}}, are continuous. For each
fixed tuple of frames the change on each edge is left and right
Haar translation, including on loops. By \ref{fdip-00MR} it
preserves Haar on that edge. Changing the independent link
variables first and then integrating over the site frames proves
the product probability assertion.}

\p{The gauge taking the scalar to its reference value reduces the
weight and observable exactly as in \ref{fdip-010Y}. They no longer
depend on the frames. Each remaining site integral of the constant
one is one. All integrands are continuous on compact spaces, so
these iterated integrals give the stated identities.}
